findings.body:
The new assertion in `BlockedWebSocketCompressionTests.EmbeddingServerAppInsideIframe_WithCompressionEnabled_Fails` requires *both* regexes to match the same log line. The two patterns describe different browser message formats ("Refused to frame" vs "Framing ... violates"), so no single console message can satisfy both. The assertion will fail on every browser, and the failure message says "match one of", which contradicts the code.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The intent is clearly "either old or new browser wording", which is a logical OR. Changing the operator fixes it.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-109"}
Assert.True(
    ParseErrorMessageRegexOld.IsMatch(logs[0].Message) ||
    ParseErrorMessageRegexNew.IsMatch(logs[0].Message),

In `ReceiveAsync`, `_receiveTcs` is now replaced before the zero-byte early return. Previously the zero-byte path returned without resetting, so the completed TCS still held the message and the next non-zero read delivered it. Now a zero-byte read consumes the message: it resets the TCS, returns only the metadata, and discards the payload. The next non-zero read then waits on a fresh TCS that only fills when the pump receives another frame, so the original message is never delivered and the reconnect test can hang or fail. This change is not required by the dependency bump, and the original comment ("Handle zero-byte reads") shows the author meant zero-byte reads to leave state alone.

::diff{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="406-417"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If the reorder was intended to fix a race, the PR should say so and justify it. Otherwise restore the original ordering so the reset happens only after a real payload copy.

::suggestion{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="406-417"}
public override async Task<WebSocketReceiveResult> ReceiveAsync(ArraySegment<byte> buffer, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    var res = await _receiveTcs.Task;
    // Handle zero-byte reads
    if (buffer.Count == 0)
    {
        return res.Item1;
    }
    _receiveTcs = new(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
    res.Item2.CopyTo(buffer);
    return res.Item1;
}

The QuarantinedTest attributes on `CanSendAndReceiveUserMessagesFromMultipleConnectionsWithSameUser` (issue 59991) and `CanSendAndReceiveUserMessagesUserNameWithPatternIsTreatedAsLiteral` (issue 63582), the reordered DisposeAsync calls, and the WebSocketWrapper edits have nothing to do with the Arcade bump. The PR description does not mention them. Quarantining a test changes which tests gate CI, so this should be reviewed on its own merits, ideally in a separate PR linked to those issues.

::diff{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="89-94"}

::callout{type="info"}
The `QuarantinedTest` constructor takes a reason string, so these calls compile. The concern is process, not correctness.

